{"generation_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9","teaching_plan":{"arc":"The lesson opens on the class bean plant growing by the window despite being fed nothing, turning its growth into a puzzle about where living things get food. It then builds the producer idea by naming leaves and chlorophyll, and it traces light, water, and carbon dioxide becoming food and oxygen inside the bean plant. It places two wrong food-source models — food from soil and sunlight eaten directly — against the producer model, showing why each fails. The close checks whether the learner recognizes that plants are producers that make their own food.","sections":[{"blocks":[{"id":"orient-b1","brief":"Introduce the class bean plant in its clear cup near the window. Point out that over several days it has added leaves and height even though no one puts food into the cup. State the question directly: where does the plant's food come from? Do not answer, name leaves, or mention photosynthesis yet.","intent":"orient","evidence":"The objective requires recognizing plants as food makers, but the opening first makes the food source feel indeterminate, and the anchor bean plant supplies the concrete case that raises the question without pre-empting the explanation.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","anchor.anchor-1","prior-2","slot.orient.purpose"],"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[]},{"id":"orient-b2","brief":"Recall two prerequisite facts and connect them to the bean plant: plants have roots, stems, and leaves, and living things need food to grow. Ask the learner to say what the bean plant already has for taking in water and air, and to predict which part could do the food-making work. Do not name photosynthesis yet.","intent":"activate-prior-knowledge","evidence":"The anchor growth claim only becomes puzzling if the learner holds that living things need food and that plants have the parts listed in prior-1 and prior-2; activating both supplies the prerequisite structure for the producer question.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["prior-1","prior-2","anchor.anchor-1","slot.orient.purpose"],"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[]}],"slot_id":"orient","transition":null,"specific_purpose":"Open on the bean plant and make its growth puzzling: living things need food, yet the plant grows while no one feeds it. This invests the learner in the producer concept."},{"blocks":[{"id":"explain-b1","brief":"Define a producer as a living thing that makes its own food, and state that plants are producers. Apply the term directly to the bean plant: because it is a producer, it does not wait to be given food. Keep the definition at the level of the plant making food for itself; do not mention chloroplasts, glucose, or chemical equations.","intent":"define","evidence":"The objective's core claim is that plants are producers, so the first explanatory move must establish the term on the anchor plant rather than merely acknowledge the word producer in the abstract.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","scope.must_establish.must-1","anchor.anchor-1","slot.explain.purpose"],"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[]},{"id":"explain-b2","brief":"Name the leaf as the part of the bean plant where the plant makes food, and chlorophyll as the substance in the leaf that captures light. State both roles plainly: the leaf is the food-making site; chlorophyll catches light. Direct attention to the bean plant's leaf as the part that faces the window light. Do not yet explain what happens after light is captured.","intent":"name-parts","evidence":"The scope requires leaves as the site and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance, and the anchor makes both observable on the window-facing bean plant where the leaf receives light.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["scope.must_establish.must-2","anchor.anchor-1","slot.explain.purpose"],"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[]},{"id":"explain-b3","brief":"Trace the flow of materials in the bean plant in order: light reaches chlorophyll in the leaf; the plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air; the plant takes in water through its roots; the plant combines light, carbon dioxide, and water to make food; oxygen leaves the plant; the food stays in the plant. State the direction of each material: water enters through roots, carbon dioxide enters the leaf from the air, oxygen exits, food remains in the plant. Do not collapse the process into a summary, do not mention limiting factors, and do not identify glucose.","intent":"trace-flow","evidence":"After naming leaves and chlorophyll as parts, the learner must follow the materials through the anchor plant to see how food and oxygen are produced, which is exactly the flow described in scope.must_establish.must-3.","position":2,"evidence_refs":["scope.must_establish.must-3","anchor.anchor-1","prior-1","slot.explain.purpose"],"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[]}],"slot_id":"explain","transition":"The bean plant's growth now stands as an unanswered need for food, so the learner is ready to learn which plant parts and materials make that food.","specific_purpose":"Build the producer model: first define producer and state that plants are producers; then name the leaf as the site and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance; finally trace how light, water, and carbon dioxide become food and oxygen in the bean plant."},{"blocks":[{"id":"contrast-b1","brief":"Surface the belief that plants get their food from the soil by pointing to the bean plant's clear cup. A learner holding this belief should say the plant is feeding from the soil because its roots are in it. Test that answer against the anchor: the soil level has not disappeared the way food would, while the plant has added leaves; the cup shows roots in the soil but no food being taken from it. State that the soil is not the source of the plant's food; it supplies water and materials the plant takes up. Do not leave the learner with the idea that soil does nothing.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"M1 is a named held belief rather than a slip, so the block must name the soil-as-food model and test it against the clear-cup bean plant where the soil remains while the plant grows, exactly as the contrast slot requires.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["M1","anchor.anchor-1","scope.must_establish.must-1","slot.contrast.purpose"],"departure_reason":"The contrast slot's typical intents do not include diagnose-misconception, but M1 is a declared wrong belief that needs to be surfaced and tested against the anchor; classify or compare would treat it as a mere alternative rather than a model to reject.","source_question_ids":[]},{"id":"contrast-b2","brief":"Surface the belief that plants eat sunlight directly as their food. A learner holding this belief should say the sunbeam itself is the plant's meal. Test that answer against the anchor's leaf: the sunlight reaching the bean plant's leaf is captured by chlorophyll, but it is not the food; the food is made by the plant using light together with carbon dioxide and water. State that light is essential but is the energy source, not the meal. Do not let the learner think light is unimportant.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"M2 is a separate held belief about light being eaten, so it must be named and corrected against the already-taught role of light and chlorophyll rather than folded silently into the soil misconception.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["M2","scope.must_establish.must-4","anchor.anchor-1","slot.contrast.purpose"],"departure_reason":"The contrast slot's typical intents do not include diagnose-misconception, but M2 is a declared wrong belief that needs to be distinguished from the target model; treating it as a comparison would wrongly place it alongside the model as a legitimate alternative.","source_question_ids":[]}],"slot_id":"contrast","transition":"The learner now knows the producer model with leaves, chlorophyll, and the input-to-output flow, so they are ready to test that model against the two common wrong answers.","specific_purpose":"Hold two wrong food-source models against the producer model: soil as food, and sunlight eaten directly. The learner must see why each fails the flow and site already built in the explanation."},{"blocks":[{"id":"check-b1","brief":"Present the approved assessment item that checks recognition of the main objective: plants are producers that make their own food. Do not add teaching, do not re-explain the process, and do not introduce a new plant example. Use only the approved item.","intent":"check-understanding","evidence":"The slot's purpose is the shared diagnostic written from the objective, so the block must measure recognition that plants are producers making their own food, not whether the learner can restate the flow or parts again.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","slot.check.purpose"],"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":["7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i1"]}],"slot_id":"check","transition":"With both wrong models rejected, the learner is ready to be checked on the core recognition that plants make their own food.","specific_purpose":"Administer the shared diagnostic to determine whether the learner recognizes the main objective claim: plants are producers that make their own food."}],"anchor_usage":{"check":"The shared diagnostic uses the approved assessment item to check the objective directly; it does not require the anchor.","orient":"Introduce the bean plant in its clear cup near the window, framing its growth as a puzzle: the plant adds leaves and height while no one gives it food.","explain":"Use the bean plant's leaves to locate light capture, then trace light, water, and carbon dioxide into the plant and name food and oxygen as outputs. The plant is the concrete site where producer work happens.","confront":"","contrast":"Return to the bean plant's clear cup and soil. Test the belief that soil feeds the plant against the visible soil level, and test the belief that sunlight is eaten directly against what light actually does in the leaf."},"misconception_focus_ids":["M1","M2"]},"teaching_validation":{"ok":true,"issues":[]},"teaching_qc":[],"teaching_review":{"status":"approved","revision":2,"reviewed_at":"2026-08-16T17:43:10.279121+00:00","reviewed_by":"2f297058-f6bc-47bf-9ffd-3208ad8246d4","teacher_note":"Approved"},"lesson_packet":{"scope":{"terminology":[],"must_establish":[{"id":"must-1","statement":"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)"},{"id":"must-2","statement":"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance"},{"id":"must-3","statement":"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen"},{"id":"must-4","statement":"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis"}],"must_not_introduce":[{"id":"exclude-1","statement":"chloroplast organelles"},{"id":"exclude-2","statement":"chemical equations of photosynthesis (e.g., 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂)"},{"id":"exclude-3","statement":"glucose as a specific sugar"},{"id":"exclude-4","statement":"light-dependent and light-independent reactions"},{"id":"exclude-5","statement":"limiting factors of photosynthesis"}]},"slots":[{"purpose":"Give the learner a reason to care and a concrete situation to hold.","slot_id":"orient","max_blocks":2,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["orient","activate-prior-knowledge","emphasise"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"Build the idea itself. State it, ground it, make it usable.","slot_id":"explain","max_blocks":3,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["explain-cause","explain","define","show-structure","trace-flow","compare","name-parts"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"Put a case and a near-miss side by side. The learner must see the boundary of the idea, not just the idea.\n","slot_id":"contrast","max_blocks":3,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["compare","classify","explain","warn","show-structure"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"The shared diagnostic. Identical across every variant. Written from the card only, never from the prose above it.\n","slot_id":"check","max_blocks":1,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["check-understanding","practise-independent","diagnose-misconception"],"visual_required":false}],"anchor":{"id":"anchor-1","description":"A classroom bean plant growing in a clear cup near a window, observed over several days as it stays…"},"lesson":{"subject":"Science","objective":"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food","grade_level":"Grade 4","lesson_mode":"first_exposure","knowledge_type":"conceptual","path_lesson_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9"},"limits":{"max_sections":4,"max_total_blocks":10,"max_blocks_per_section":3},"resource_id":"lesson","approved_items":[{"id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i1","stem":"A student adds nothing but distilled water to a sealed jar containing only a small green plant and a soil substitute with zero nutrients. After several weeks in sunlight, the plant has grown larger. Where did most of the new plant matter come from?","card_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It came from the water, which the plant drinks to build its body parts.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"It came from a gas in the air that the plant uses to build its own food.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"It came from the sunlight, which the plant eats as its main food supply.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"It came from an invisible dust in the air that plants need for their nutrients.","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":null,"c":"M2","d":null},"correct_key":"b"},{"id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i2","stem":"A desert cactus grows in pure, washed sand that contains no measurable nutrients. A gardener adds nothing but light and water for a year, and the cactus doubles in mass. What was the main source of its new material?","card_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The sand, which the cactus roots absorb as its main food source.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"The light, which the cactus swallows and stores as plant food.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"A gas from the air that the cactus uses to manufacture its own food.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"Water alone, which turns into solid plant tissue as the cactus grows.","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":"M2","c":null,"d":null},"correct_key":"c"},{"id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i3","stem":"Two identical seedlings are planted. Seedling A is placed on a windowsill in bright sunlight. Seedling B is placed in a dark closet. Both get the same soil and water. After two weeks, Seedling A is larger and greener. Why did sunlight help Seedling A grow?","card_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It provided the energy needed for the plant to make its own food.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"It acted as the food itself, which the plant absorbed while growing.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"It warmed the soil, making it release more food into the roots.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"It made the plant stronger without affecting the plant's actual food.","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M2","c":"M1","d":null},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i4","stem":"A scientist observes a green pond plant in a sealed glass container of nutrient-free water under a bright lamp. Bubbles form on its leaves as it grows. The bubbles are oxygen gas produced during the plant's food-making process. Which statement best explains what the plant is doing?","card_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It is turning sunlight directly into its food while releasing oxygen.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"It is soaking up dissolved nutrients from the water to make its body.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"It is using light energy to combine a gas from the water with a gas from air.","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"It is making its own food using light, water, and carbon dioxide from air.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M2","b":"M1","c":null,"d":null},"correct_key":"d"},{"id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i5","stem":"A farmer notices that vegetables in a field with poor, rocky soil still grow well in full sun and rain. The rocks provide very few nutrients. What conclusion best fits this observation?","card_id":"7d2e4b2b-04bd-49f5-a860-2fe0d70c5ea9:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The vegetables must have eaten tiny rock particles from the poor soil.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"The vegetables made their own food using sunlight, water, and air.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"The vegetables swallowed the sunlight and turned it into solid food.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"The vegetables did not need any food as long as they received water.","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":null,"c":"M2","d":null},"correct_key":"b"}],"misconceptions":[{"id":"M1","statement":"{'id': 'M1', 'description': 'Plants get their food from the soil.', 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"M2","statement":"{'id': 'M2', 'description': 'Plants eat sunlight directly as their food.', 'source': 'drafted'}"}],"prior_established":[{"id":"prior-1","statement":"plants have roots, stems and leaves"},{"id":"prior-2","statement":"living things need food to grow"}]},"catalogue":{"version":"1.1.0","form_projection_hash":"05fddc80790c232176df5c03bf7ea1ead8b910d06c848b4836be205a7552c98b","teaching_projection_hash":"a9530874d702efc409dcd4c87658fe037981b8a8a6ed76e1d95a551ff8885870"}}